/// Whether an unresolved value is a marker a clause fails closed on, as against a value the document lacks
/// or an enumeration over an empty collection, which are decided. Every kind is named, with no wildcard
/// arm, so a kind added later fails to compile here until it is classified. Both undecidables answer
/// true whether or not the marker is a refusal: a cell that has to tell a refusal from a value nobody
/// could decide reads `UnResolvedKind::is_refusal` itself.
fn fails_closed(kind: &UnResolvedKind) -> bool {
    match kind {
        UnResolvedKind::Absent | UnResolvedKind::EmptyEnumeration => false,
        UnResolvedKind::Undecidable { .. } => true,
    }
}

// # Counting what this file's tables cover
//
// A table-driven test's coverage is counted in cells, not in grep hits. `grep -c` counts lines, and the
// two agree only when every table has exactly one row. The assertion that an empty-selection skip names
// the query that matched nothing appears at two sites, but as five loop cells across two tests:
// `an_empty_selection_skip_says_which_query_matched_nothing`, whose table is `[_; 4]`, and
// `a_type_block_skip_names_the_cause_it_can_support`, where it is cell 2 of 4. "Both tests" is right and
// "all five cells" is right; "both cells" is neither.
//
// Each loop table in this file pins its length in its binding type -- `let cases: [_; 10] = [..]` -- so
// a deleted cell becomes `error[E0308]` instead of a silent shrink, and
// `every_loop_table_carries_its_length` walks the file for tables without one. Corroborate a population
// by making the test state it rather than by adding to a remembered number: strip one `: [_; N]`
// annotation and the failure message names both the count the rule walked and the offending line. The
// count is deliberately not reproduced here, because it moves every time a test adds a table, and a
// figure invalidated by the next change is a maintenance trap wearing the costume of precision.
//
// Suite totals are the same kind of figure. The total moves with every test added anywhere in the
// crate, and `src/lib.rs` and `src/main.rs` each build this file and `parser_tests.rs`, so a
// crate-internal test function counts twice. Reconciling test FUNCTIONS and `#[case]` attributes
// against a diff is what makes a wrong total visible; comparing one total against another does not,
// because a wrong total can be internally consistent. No total is stated in this file as a current
// figure.
//
// A reference to a test by name resolves to a test or it does not, and counting the ones that do not is
// a measurement of the net rather than of the tree. Over the backticked snake_case tokens on comment
// lines in `guard/src` and `guard/tests`, a deliberately wide resolution net still leaves 99 of 358
// distinct tokens unmatched, and that residue is external-crate methods (`read_to_string`,
// `min_by_key`, `try_parse_from`), clap attributes (`default_value_ifs`, `arg_required_else_help`),
// rstest's generated `case_N_` labels, and cell names that exist only inside string literals. A bare
// "referenced but not found" count says nothing until that residue is classified.
//
// # What is deliberately not restated
//
// The clause-sweep figures. The 94-, 140-, 210-, 235- and 432-clause sweeps and the 1080-shape grid
// never had their clause lists committed, so no figure in any of them can be re-derived or falsified
// from a checkout. `eval.rs` already records that for the rows it dates; nothing here repeats one of
// those numbers as though a reader could check it.

/// Collects one table's per-cell disagreements so that a failing cell cannot hide the rest.
///
/// Why this exists rather than a per-iteration `assert_eq!`. That form aborts its loop at the first
/// mismatch, so a table of N cells reports one label and says nothing about the other N-1. Measured with
/// the walk in `RootScope`/`BlockScope::variable_binding_query` reverted: across
/// `a_parameter_query_root_is_judged_against_the_callers_binding` and
/// `a_let_chain_is_followed_in_the_scope_that_wrote_it` the run is `1489 passed; 2 failed`, and exactly 2
/// of those 25 cells name themselves -- the first of each table.
///
/// That loses the diagnostic these tables are built to carry. Every one of them is a set of pairs: a
/// shadowed spelling beside an unshadowed control, a chain beside its literal oracle, an over-wide cell
/// beside the narrow one. Which cells move *together* is the finding; a repair that fixes one spelling and
/// breaks its control is the exact failure these catch, and aborting on the first cell shows one half of
/// one pair.
///
/// Comparison happens here rather than at the call site so a caller cannot reintroduce the abort by
/// passing an already-evaluated `bool`. `assert_all_agreed` takes `self` so a table cannot be checked and
/// then silently left unasserted.
///
/// Known limitation: this covers tables whose cell is one verdict. The four `SKIP`-reason tables around
/// `a_type_block_skip_names_the_cause_it_can_support` assert a verdict *and* several properties of the
/// recorded reason, and they also abort inside the loop through `unwrap_or_else(|| panic!(..))`. They have
/// the same defect and need a wider shape than this; they are untouched here.
#[derive(Default)]
struct CellFailures {
    seen: usize,
    disagreed: Vec<String>,
}

impl CellFailures {
    fn check<T: PartialEq + std::fmt::Debug>(&mut self, label: &str, expected: T, actual: T) {
        self.seen += 1;
        if expected != actual {
            self.disagreed
                .push(format!("{label}: wanted {expected:?}, got {actual:?}"));
        }
    }

    /// `check` with a diagnostic (a record dump, say) built ONLY when the cell disagrees, so an agreeing
    /// table formats nothing and a red cell still names what it read.
    fn check_with<T: PartialEq + std::fmt::Debug>(
        &mut self,
        label: &str,
        expected: T,
        actual: T,
        diagnostic: impl FnOnce() -> String,
    ) {
        self.seen += 1;
        if expected != actual {
            self.disagreed.push(format!(
                "{label}: wanted {expected:?}, got {actual:?}; {}",
                diagnostic()
            ));
        }
    }

    /// A table that is always concluded: `f` fills it, and whatever `f` returns the helper notes pending
    /// at its end (`HELPER_NOTES`) are read into the verdict. An `Ok` runs `assert_all_agreed`, so a
    /// pending note fails the test; an `Err` surfaces as it is, the notes printed beside it, so an early
    /// `?` return never hides its own error behind the notes. The one shape a table needs so that no
    /// exit skips its conclusion; a table built with `CellFailures::default` relies on its author calling
    /// `assert_all_agreed` (the `Drop` guard only prints what such a table left).
    fn table(f: impl FnOnce(&mut CellFailures) -> Result<()>) -> Result<()> {
        let mut cells = CellFailures::default();
        match f(&mut cells) {
            Ok(()) => {
                cells.assert_all_agreed();
                Ok(())
            }
            Err(error) => {
                let noted = HELPER_NOTES.with(|notes| std::mem::take(&mut *notes.borrow_mut()));
                if !noted.is_empty() {
                    eprintln!(
                        "the table returned an error with helper notes pending:\n  {}",
                        noted.join("\n  ")
                    );
                }
                Err(error)
            }
        }
    }

    fn assert_all_agreed(mut self) {
        // The defects the test's helpers noted while the table was filled (`HELPER_NOTES`), each one a
        // disagreeing cell of this table: a helper called from a closure the rows call has no handle on
        // the table, and asserting there aborted the test at its first call with every other cell hidden.
        let noted = HELPER_NOTES.with(|notes| std::mem::take(&mut *notes.borrow_mut()));
        for defect in noted {
            self.seen += 1;
            self.disagreed.push(format!("a helper noted: {defect}"));
        }
        // Arguments spelled out rather than captured inline, for the edition-2018 reason given on the
        // skip-reason tests below: a lone literal handed to `assert!` becomes the payload verbatim.
        assert!(
            self.disagreed.is_empty(),
            "{} of {} cells disagree:\n  {}",
            self.disagreed.len(),
            self.seen,
            self.disagreed.join("\n  ")
        );
    }
}

/// A table dropped with helper notes still pending (`HELPER_NOTES`) prints them to stderr instead of
/// letting a reworded bind line go unread, and lets the test's own result surface: the one way a table
/// is dropped before `assert_all_agreed` reads it is an early `?` return, whose `Err` the harness
/// reports, and a panic here replaced that error with the notes, hiding what failed. Not during an
/// unwind, where a second panic would abort the test binary. A `Drop` cannot tell an early `Err` from a
/// function that returned `Ok` without concluding its table, so the notes are printed in both cases and
/// never asserted here; a table that reaches `assert_all_agreed` takes them into its own verdict.
impl Drop for CellFailures {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        if std::thread::panicking() {
            return;
        }
        let pending = HELPER_NOTES.with(|notes| std::mem::take(&mut *notes.borrow_mut()));
        if pending.is_empty() {
            return;
        }
        eprintln!(
            "a cell table was dropped with helper notes no table read (the test returned before \
             `assert_all_agreed`; its own error, if any, follows):\n  {}",
            pending.join("\n  ")
        );
    }
}

