/// The empty-selection answer reaches only the document's own population.
///
/// Invariant: an absent or empty collection with a filter still ahead reports SKIP only when it is the
/// document's own population: `Resources` absent, `Resources.*` over `{}`, `Resources[ ... ]` with the
/// filter directly on the key, the same key behind a leading `this.`, `%r.*`, `%r[ ... ]` or
/// `%r[*][ ... ]` where `let r = Resources` or `let r = this.Resources` is a plain alias of that key
/// bound at the document (wherever the reference sits, a block clause included), a parameterized
/// rule's parameter whose argument was that key or that alias, or the population filtered down to
/// nothing. Every other absent or empty collection keeps the unresolved marker and the operator
/// decides, which is FAIL for `== 'prod'`: below a filter that ran, below a wildcard or variable
/// descent, inside a type block or a block clause, through a `%var` bound to anything but a top-level
/// key (a nested path, a function result that shadows such an alias, a parameter whose argument was a
/// nested path), and along a path of keys to a nested collection (`Resources.R.Properties.Tags`,
/// `planned_values.root_module.resources`, `spec.template.spec.containers`). The fix extends only to
/// the top-level population; nested populations, Terraform's and Kubernetes' included, keep upstream's
/// FAIL. A filter that runs over a present collection and keeps nothing is a different thing and
/// reports SKIP at every depth. The rows named `upstream_*` are verdicts this fix leaves exactly where
/// upstream main has them; `key_filter_right_hand_query_without_names_fails_closed` is the one row of
/// its group without the prefix, because a key filter whose right-hand side does not resolve now fails
/// closed where upstream reports SKIP, and the row says so in its name rather than claiming parity.
///
/// Two filter spellings appear, and on this base they select alike. `Tags[ Key == 'Name' ]` runs its
/// predicate against each element and selects: the `upstream_bare_filter_*` rows show the full set of
/// verdicts, an absent collection FAIL, an empty one SKIP, unrelated entries SKIP, a matching entry
/// PASS or FAIL by its value. `Tags[*][ Key == 'Name' ]` enumerates the list first and then runs the
/// same predicate against each element (on upstream main a filter that follows an enumeration
/// evaluated its predicate against the scope the enumerating query runs in and kept nothing, so any
/// non-empty list SKIPped there); over an absent or empty list the enumeration itself fails, which is
/// why the rows written with `[*][ ... ]` pin FAIL for those documents while the bare spelling SKIPs
/// over `[]`. The places where the answer below the population depends on which spelling was used, or
/// on unrelated data, are recorded apart from this invariant, in
/// `known_upstream_inconsistencies_below_the_population`.
///
/// The parameter rows carry a second clause, `Parameters exists`, because on upstream a parameterized
/// rule whose whole body SKIPs is failed closed by an asserting caller; beside a passing clause, the
/// body's verdict is PASS when the population clause SKIPs and FAIL when it does not, and the control
/// row writes the same two clauses out without a parameter. The `*_call_of_a_population_rule_*` rows
/// pin the two call spellings themselves on this base, which has no arm that fails a skipped rule
/// closed for an asserting caller: an asserted `check(Resources)` reports SKIP over `{}` and over
/// `{"Resources":{}}` alike, as `when check(Resources)` does over both (on upstream the asserted
/// spelling reports FAIL on both documents, because the invoked clause fails there). The population
/// answer changes what the invoked rule reports, never what a caller does with a SKIP.
///
/// Rows whose document is `[]`, `"x"`, or otherwise not a map pin the alias and the parameter against
/// the written-out clause: `Resources` cannot be looked up in a list or a scalar, and that unresolved
/// marker is a retrieval failure for every spelling, not an empty population, so `%r.*[ ... ]` fails
/// there like `Resources.*[ ... ]` does. The negated call rows read that failure through `not`: the
/// invoked rule fails, so the negated asserting call passes, where a skipped invoked rule would have
/// failed closed. A document that is an empty file cannot be built, so the command line pins that
/// case instead (both binaries refuse the file).
///
/// Each of the three early-return sites has a FAIL row that only it can turn red: the key site
/// (`absent_tags_under_a_selected_bucket`), the list site (`empty_tags_under_a_selected_bucket`), the
/// map site (`empty_map_under_a_selected_resource_wildcard_spelling`).
#[rstest::rstest]
#[case::absent_tags_under_a_selected_bucket(TRAILING_FILTER_RULE, UNTAGGED_BUCKET, Status::FAIL)]
